#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 3 Creative Arts Written Core batch 03: drama stories, props, audience, elements of dance w13-l1 through w18-l2 (12)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, DRA, DAN

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y3-w13-l1', DRA, ['a story beginning', 'a problem', 'a resolution', 'a scene'],
  "A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens.",
  "Example: 'The Lost Lantern'. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search together, argue, then work as a team. Resolution: they find the lantern under a log and share their dinner. Plan: Who? Two children. Where? Forest. Problem? Lantern lost. Solution? Teamwork and a careful search.",
  "Plan a short drama about a missing football at the park. Write the who, where, problem and resolution in order.",
  "Strong answer: 'Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.' It has a beginning, a clear problem and a satisfying end.",
  "Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events.",
  "Make a story plan card for a drama of your own with a title, characters, setting, problem and resolution. Add one line of narration and two lines of dialogue. Tell your plan to a family member and ask what they think will happen next, to see whether the problem is clear."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w13-l2', DRA, ['dialogue', 'narrator', 'a cue', 'a rehearsal'],
  "To act out a story, divide the parts: narrator, characters and a person to give cues. Dialogue is spoken by characters, and the narrator links the scenes. A cue is a signal for the next person to start, such as the last words of the line before. Rehearsing means repeating the scenes to remember the order, improve the voices and fix mistakes. Speak clearly and face the audience, and keep your body safe by agreeing on any action before you act. If you forget the line, say it in your own words and keep going.",
  "Example: Mia is the narrator: 'The two children searched under every log.' Ben, as a child, says, 'It must be near the stream!' His cue is Mia's last word, 'log'. They rehearse three times, checking that Ben starts on the cue and speaks clearly at the audience. In the second rehearsal Ben forgets and says, 'We should look by the water', which works.",
  "Your friend always comes in too early on stage. Write one way to help them find the cue and one way to recover if they forget a line.",
  "Strong answer: 'Agree that their cue is the last word of my line, and say it slowly. If they forget their line, they can say it in their own words and keep the story going.' A clear cue plus a recovery plan helps a team perform confidently.",
  "Believing that actors must remember every word exactly. Knowing the story and staying in character matters more, and you can recover from mistakes by keeping the meaning.",
  "Write a one-page script of three scenes for your story from last lesson, with a narrator and two characters. Mark each cue. Rehearse it three times with a family member and write one thing that improved each time."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w14-l1', DRA, ['the stage space', 'a prop', 'a costume', 'a level'],
  "Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.",
  "Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.",
  "In a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have.",
  "Strong answer: 'Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.' The lion's strong position and high level show pride, and the bear's low and back position shows shyness.",
  "Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter.",
  "Choose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and write the reason for each choice. Perform the scene using things you already have at home and keep walkways clear."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w14-l2', DRA, ['a costume piece', 'recycled materials', 'a safe prop', 'a design plan'],
  "Props and costumes can be made from recycled materials such as cardboard, paper, fabric scraps and string. A design plan shows what the item is, which materials it uses and how it will be made. Keep props light and soft with no sharp points, and never use things that can hurt others. Use scissors carefully, tape securely and ask an adult for help with anything tricky. A good costume piece is simple and shows a key trait of the character, like a gold crown for a king or a feather for a bird.",
  "Example: Jin designs a dragon mask. Plan: cardboard base, green paper scales glued in rows, a red paper tongue and elastic string. The mask is lightweight and has eye holes big enough to see. He cuts the shapes with scissors, folding the edges so nothing is sharp. The green scales and red tongue show 'dragon' from far away.",
  "Design a simple crown for a character who is generous and kind. List the materials, one safety choice and one decoration that shows the trait.",
  "Strong answer: 'Cardboard band with rounded gold paper points and a paper heart in the middle to show kindness. Materials: cardboard, gold paper, glue and tape. Safety: round points, not sharp.' The decoration is linked to the character trait.",
  "Thinking that a costume has to be very detailed or bought from a shop. Simple recycled materials can show a character just as well, as long as it is safe and easy to move in.",
  "Make a prop and a costume piece for a character using recycled materials. Draw a design plan first with materials and safety choices. Wear them in a short scene and write one improvement. Ask an adult to help with scissors and glue."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w15-l1', DRA, ['an audience', 'projection', 'facing out', 'a bow'],
  "When you perform for an audience, you need to be heard and seen. Projection means speaking clearly and loudly enough for the back of the room without shouting. Face the audience, or at least turn your body partly out, so people can see your face. Pause between lines so the audience can react. Good audience members sit quietly, watch carefully, laugh in the right places and clap at the end. After the performance, performers bow together. It is normal to be nervous, and slow breaths can help.",
  "Example: Tessa speaks her line, 'The treasure is buried here!', using a clear voice that reaches the back row, with her body angled toward the audience. She waits for a moment of silence before the next line. The audience laughs when the pirate trips and then sits quietly during the tense part. At the end, all actors bow together.",
  "A performer speaks too quietly and faces the back wall. Write two changes that would help the audience, and one thing the audience should do.",
  "Strong answer: 'Turn to face the audience and speak louder from your belly, taking a slow breath first. The audience should listen quietly and clap at the end.' Facing out and projection make performers easier to hear, and a kind audience supports them.",
  "Thinking that a loud voice means shouting. Projection means a clear, strong voice from a relaxed body, and shouting can strain your throat and make the words unclear.",
  "Perform a one-minute scene for family members. Before you start, write three goals such as facing out, projecting and pausing. Afterwards, ask for two stars and a wish and record which goal you met. Practise slow breathing if you feel nervous."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w15-l2', DRA, ['a showcase', 'a performance review', 'two stars and a wish', 'a reflection'],
  "At the end of a drama unit, students share their best scene and reflect on what they learned. A performance review looks at role, voice, body, use of space, props and audience connection. Give feedback as two stars, which are things that worked well, and a wish, which is one change. A reflection names what you did well, what was challenging and what you will practise next. Treat every performer with respect, and speak about the performance rather than the person.",
  "Example: After the scene, Kai writes: 'Star: I used a low slow voice for the giant. Star: I stayed in role the whole time. Wish: I will face the audience more.' His teacher says, 'Star: your big steps showed the giant. Wish: pause before the last line.' Kai's reflection says he will practise facing out.",
  "Write two stars and a wish about a scene you saw or performed this unit, using a drama term in each.",
  "Strong answer: 'Star: the narrator's clear voice told the story well. Star: the kneeling level showed the sad character. Wish: use a prop to show where the cave is.' Each comment names a drama element and is about the performance.",
  "Thinking that feedback should only say what was wrong. Good feedback names what worked, which tells the performer what to keep, and gives one clear idea to improve.",
  "Prepare your best scene from the unit and perform it for family. Ask them for two stars and a wish using the words role, voice, body, space or prop. Write a reflection of five sentences about your strengths and a next step."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w16-l1', DAN, ['body', 'space', 'time', 'energy'],
  "Dance is made from elements. Body is what moves, such as arms, legs and torso, and the shapes you make. Space is where you move: high, middle or low levels, big or small movements and directions like forward, backward and sideways. Time is when and how fast you move, including beat, tempo and pauses. Energy is how you move: smooth, sharp, heavy or light. Dancers choose these elements to create a mood. Dance in a clear space with safe footwear, and stop if anything hurts.",
  "Example: a dance about a leaf blowing in the wind. Body: arms curve and a spin. Space: moves from low to high, and travels sideways across the room. Time: a slow start, then faster, then a stop. Energy: light and floaty. The elements together show the leaf blowing away. If the energy changed to heavy and sharp, it would look like a rock.",
  "A dance shows a snail crossing a path. Choose a body shape, a level, a speed and an energy for it.",
  "Strong answer: 'Body: curled round like a shell. Level: low near the floor. Time: very slow. Energy: smooth and heavy.' These choices match the snail, whereas a high, quick and sharp movement would look like a different creature.",
  "Thinking that dance is only about steps. Dance uses all of the elements, and changing one element, such as the energy or level, can change the whole meaning.",
  "Choose three things: a robot, a feather and a storm. For each write a body shape, a space choice, a speed and an energy. Try each with movement in a clear space or describe it with a drawing, and tell someone what changes between them."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w16-l2', DAN, ['a level', 'a direction', 'a movement idea', 'exploring'],
  "Exploring movement means trying many different ways to move before choosing the best ones. Choose a movement idea such as stretch, twist, shake or spin, and explore it at high, middle and low levels, in different directions, and at fast and slow times. Then choose the version that best shows your idea. Make sure there is plenty of room, no slippery socks and an adult nearby. Always move at a level that is comfortable for your body, and you can explore the same idea using only your arms or hands.",
  "Example: Jo explores 'twist'. High: twist the upper body with arms overhead. Low: twist while crouching. Forward: twist and step ahead. Slow: twist as if moving through honey. Quick: twist as if shaking off water. She chooses the low slow twist because it looks like a vine growing, which is her idea for the dance.",
  "Explore the idea 'stretch' in three ways: a level, a direction and a speed. Say which one you would keep for a dance about a plant growing and why.",
  "Strong answer: 'Stretch high, stretch sideways and stretch slowly. For a plant I would keep high and slow, because plants grow upward and gradually.' Choosing from several options and giving a reason shows exploring, not just copying.",
  "Believing that the first movement idea is always the best. Dancers explore many ideas first, and often the third or fourth idea is the most interesting.",
  "Pick three movement words (such as swing, curl and jump) and explore each at three levels and two speeds. Write which version you chose for each and why. Show a family member your favourite, or draw stick figures if movement is not possible."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w17-l1', DAN, ['a locomotor movement', 'a non-locomotor movement', 'a shape', 'a pathway'],
  "Dancers move in two main ways. Locomotor movements travel from place to place, such as walking, skipping, hopping, galloping and sliding. Non-locomotor movements stay in one spot, such as stretching, twisting, swaying and bending. A pathway is the line you make on the floor, like a straight line, a curve or a zigzag. Dancers can also make a shape with their body, such as wide, narrow, curved or twisted. Always check for clear space, move at a speed where you can stay in control and stop if you feel dizzy.",
  "Example: in a dance about a kangaroo, Ali hops forward in a straight pathway (locomotor), stops, and then stretches tall and sways side to side (non-locomotor). Next he gallops in a curved pathway and ends in a curled shape. A list of movements: hop, stretch, sway, gallop, curl. Two are locomotor and three are non-locomotor.",
  "Sort these into locomotor or non-locomotor: skip, twist, slide, bend, march. Then add a pathway for the travelling ones.",
  "Strong answer: locomotor are skip, slide and march. Non-locomotor are twist and bend. A pathway could be a zigzag for the skip and a curve for the slide. Travelling movements move you across the floor, and non-locomotor movements stay in place.",
  "Thinking that if you stay in one place it is not dancing. Non-locomotor movements are a key part of dance, and a dance can use only movements in one spot.",
  "Make a chart with two columns for locomotor and non-locomotor movements and add at least five in each. Then link three movements into a short sequence with a pathway for each, and perform it in a clear space or draw it as a map of footprints."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w17-l2', DAN, ['travelling', 'turning', 'balancing', 'a safe landing'],
  "Travelling, turning and balancing are core dance skills. Travelling means moving across the floor with control. Turning means rotating the body, and you can make it easier by focusing on one spot on the wall and turning your head last. Balancing means holding a still shape on a small base such as one foot. Strong balance comes from tight tummy muscles, steady eyes and a slightly bent standing knee. Practise on a flat surface, wear safe footwear or bare feet, keep a hand near a wall for support and stop if dizzy.",
  "Example: Mel practises a slow turn. She picks a spot on the wall, turns her body slowly while keeping her eyes on it, then snaps her head around last. She finishes in a balance on one foot with her arms out, holds still for three seconds and lowers softly. When she wobbles, she looks at a fixed point and tightens her tummy.",
  "A dancer keeps falling out of a balance. Write two tips to help them hold still longer and one safety rule.",
  "Strong answer: 'Look at one spot that does not move and keep your tummy muscles tight. Safety: do it near a wall and on a flat floor.' A fixed focus and a strong core help balance, and a wall allows safe support.",
  "Believing that spinning faster is better. Fast uncontrolled spinning can make you dizzy and fall, whereas slow controlled turns with a spot to focus on look better and are safer.",
  "Practise a three-part sequence: travel, turn, balance. Make three versions using different movements. Rate your control from 1 to 5 each time and write a goal for each. If you cannot stand, make the sequence using arms or a seated chair version."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w18-l1', DAN, ['music mood', 'a movement response', 'a dance idea', 'energy and tempo'],
  "Music can give ideas for movement. Listen to the beat, tempo, pitch and dynamics and choose movements that match the mood. Slow, soft, low music might suit smooth, low, heavy movements, and fast, loud, high music might suit quick, sharp, high movements. You can also choose to move against the mood on purpose, as long as you can explain why. Move in a clear, safe space at a comfortable volume and listen for any pause or change in the music that tells you to change.",
  "Example: Sana listens to a slow lullaby. She chooses to sway slowly with arms curved, at a low level, and she lets her energy feel soft. When the music gets a bit louder, she grows taller. When the tempo slows at the end, she lowers to the floor and stops. Her movement follows the mood and the changes in the music.",
  "Imagine music that is fast, loud and high. Describe the body shapes, level, speed and energy you would use to match it.",
  "Strong answer: 'Quick, sharp jumps and hops at a high level, with arms stretched out and a bright energy.' These choices match the fast tempo, loud dynamics and high pitch, so the dance and music feel connected.",
  "Thinking that dancers must always move exactly the way the music sounds. Dancers can choose different responses as long as they give a reason, and silence or contrast can be just as powerful.",
  "Choose two pieces of music with different moods. For each, write the tempo, dynamics and mood, then plan movements for each using body, space, time and energy. Perform them or draw a picture of each, and say how the movement matches the music."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w18-l2', DAN, ['a steady beat', 'a rhythm pattern', 'a clap and step', 'in time'],
  "Dancing in time means moving on the beat. Find the beat by listening for the steady pulse and tapping your foot. A rhythm pattern is a repeated group of long and short movements that fits over the beat. You can show a rhythm pattern with steps, claps and stamps: for example step, step, clap. Count 1, 2, 3, 4 to stay together. If you lose the beat, stop for a moment, listen and join in again on beat one. Keep stamping soft and wear safe shoes or bare feet.",
  "Example: Max claps a steady beat and steps in a pattern of step, step, clap, rest over four beats. He says 1, 2, 3, 4 as he goes. The first step and second step each take one beat, the clap takes one beat and the rest is silent for one beat. When the music speeds up, his steps speed up evenly, so he is still in time.",
  "Create a four-beat pattern using step, clap and rest. Write it as a list, and say which beat you would count the rest on.",
  "Strong answer: 'Step, clap, step, rest. The rest happens on beat four, and I stay still but keep counting in my head.' A rest is a silent beat that still takes up time.",
  "Thinking that if you are not moving, you are out of time. A rest is part of the rhythm, and counting inside your head helps you rejoin on the right beat.",
  "Make a four-beat rhythm pattern and a different eight-beat pattern using at least three movements and one rest. Practise with a favourite song, counting aloud first. Teach one to someone at home and ask them to show if they stayed on the beat."),
]

